      title: "A Sophisticated Argument on Innovation",
      image: "",
      imageAlt: "",
      text: `The writer addresses community members divided over funding, opening with a concrete scene rather than an abstract claim. Instead of announcing the position immediately, the passage builds pressure through examples that show why the issue feels urgent to the intended audience. The tone remains controlled, but the writer's diction suggests frustration with easy answers and simple slogans.\n\nMidway through the passage, the writer introduces evidence that could support more than one conclusion. That choice forces the audience to consider not only whether the claim is reasonable, but also how the reasoning handles limits, exceptions, and competing values. The passage becomes persuasive because it links evidence to a larger purpose instead of assuming the facts will speak for themselves.\n\nBy the end, the writer uses a qualified final claim. The argument does not pretend that the problem can be solved by one action; instead, it asks the audience to accept a more careful standard for judgment.`,
